"""Author-facing replacement prose. The original manuscript is never edited."""
LABELS={4:'fig:exp_data_nd',5:'fig:exp_data_velocity',10:'fig:experimental_case_study',
    11:'fig:experimental_review_score_distribution',12:'fig:experimental_review_budget_operating_points',
    13:'fig:experimental_physical_consistency',14:'fig:experimental_scene_collision_counts',15:'fig:experimental_collision_count_map'}

CAPTIONS={
4:r'''Reprocessed droplet population statistics for the 14 experimental sequences. (a) Number-density distribution, \(dn/dD\), using 5 \(\mu\mathrm{m}\) bins. The solid curve is the arithmetic mean across sequences; shading is the pointwise 95\% interval from 50,000 sequence-bootstrap resamples. (b) Droplet count per hologram and liquid water content for each sequence, with means and one sample standard deviation across all 10,917 frames. These are the updated experimental measurements; the empirical inputs used to generate the synthetic training ensemble remain fixed at their previously specified values.''',
5:r'''Reprocessed diameter--velocity distributions for 72,047 trajectories with at least five valid observations and median diameters between 25 and 300 \(\mu\mathrm{m}\). Each trajectory contributes its median diameter and median smoothed velocity components. (a) Horizontal, (b) downward-positive vertical, and (c) axial velocity. The dashed curve is the Beard (1976) terminal velocity minus the unchanged upward-flow offset of 0.26 \(\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\). The axes show the same ranges as the earlier figure; trajectories outside these display ranges are retained in the underlying summaries. These measurements do not redefine the frozen synthetic training distribution.''',
10:r'''Representative visually confirmed collision--coalescence events reconstructed using three phase-retrieval iterations. Cases (a) and (b) show updated smoothed three-dimensional trajectories and reconstructed image sequences from raw sequences S0021 and S0017, respectively. Red and blue identify terminating and continuing tracks; triangles and squares indicate the starts and ends of the displayed trajectory segments. Open circles and short lines overlay observed particle positions and recent track histories. Times are relative to illustrative merging frames 3608 and 8563 (zero-based processing frame indices), selected from the reviewed images. These display origins do not change the raw candidate times, 3612 and 8565, used for feature extraction and the physical diagnostics. Headings give the terminating-parent, continuing-parent and post-event median diameters from those analysis windows. Each image is a 128-by-128-pixel crop, and scale bars represent 500 \(\mu\mathrm{m}\). The axial coordinate is depth relative to the reconstruction front.''',
11:r'''Final ranking-score distributions for 1,108 reviewed experimental candidates with binary judgments: 186 confirmed collision--coalescence events and 922 noncollision candidates. The 12 ambiguous candidates are omitted. All plotted scores lie within the displayed range. Overlap between the distributions means that these scores prioritize visual review rather than establish a calibrated probability of collision.''',
12:r'''Retrospective confirmed-event coverage and review precision versus the number of candidates reviewed per sequence. Candidates are ordered by final score separately within each of the 14 sequences. Coverage is the fraction of the 186 events confirmed within the top-80 cohort; precision uses all reviewed candidates, including ambiguous judgments, in its denominator. The upper axis gives the total review count. No equal integer budget gives exactly 50\% precision: 24 candidates per sequence give 173 confirmed events among 336 reviews (51.49\% precision), and 25 give the same 173 among 350 reviews (49.43\%). Both recover 93.01\% of the confirmed-event set. The shaded band marks this boundary. The dashed vertical line marks the smallest per-sequence budget attaining 95\% coverage, 34: 177 events among 476 reviews, or 95.16\% coverage and 37.18\% precision. Horizontal dotted guides indicate the requested 50\% precision and 95\% coverage thresholds. Coverage is conditional on the reviewed confirmed-event set and is not absolute experimental recall.''',
13:r'''Physical-consistency diagnostics for reviewed experimental candidates, excluding ambiguous judgments. (a) Measured post-event diameter versus the volume-conservation prediction, \(D_{\mathrm{exp}}=(D_1^3+D_2^3)^{1/3}\). (b) Measured downward-positive vertical velocity versus the mass-weighted prediction, \(v_{y,\mathrm{exp}}=(D_1^3v_{y,1}+D_2^3v_{y,2})/(D_1^3+D_2^3)\). Orange markers show 184 confirmed events with valid diameter measurements and 179 with valid velocity estimates. Blue hexagonal bins show 908 and 822 noncollision candidates, respectively, on logarithmic color scales. Diameter windows contain up to the last ten observations at or before raw candidate frame \(f-2\) and the first twenty after \(f\). Velocities are medians of observed displacement-to-frame-interval ratios within these windows; each velocity window requires at least three observations. Two confirmed events lack a required diameter window, and seven fail the velocity-window requirements. The corresponding counts for noncollision candidates are 14 and 100. Missing measurements or insufficient observed points exclude a candidate only from the affected diagnostic; one observed point may supply a diameter but cannot define an interval velocity. No residual-based outlier removal is applied. Black lines show one-to-one agreement.''',
14:r'''Confirmed collision--coalescence counts and volumetric rates compared with gravitational-kernel predictions, separately for 14 sequences and for their pooled result. Open circles show the 186 visually confirmed events without correction for missed events; filled diamonds show predictions from the reprocessed frame-wise number-density distributions. All 10,917 frames per sequence and the nominal 10.24-by-10.24-by-102.3 \(\mathrm{mm^3}\) volume are used. Error bars show conditional Poisson standard deviations, \(\sqrt{\Lambda}\), with density and kernel fixed. The pooled prediction is \(222.41\pm14.91\) events. Secondary axes give counts corresponding to the indicated exposure. The bars do not describe uncertainty from measured diameters, density estimation, model choice or missed events.''',
15:r'''Unordered parent-diameter-pair counts pooled across the 14 sequences, in 25 \(\mu\mathrm{m}\) bins over \(25\leq D<300\,\mu\mathrm{m}\). (a) The 185 confirmed events for which both parent diameters are available. One further confirmed event lacks one parent diameter and remains in the total count of 186 in Fig.~\ref{fig:experimental_scene_collision_counts}, but cannot be placed in this map. (b) Gravitational-kernel predictions, with annotations giving expected counts plus or minus conditional Poisson standard deviations, rounded to one decimal place. Each unordered bin pair is counted once. Diagonal cells integrate all pairs within the same finite-width bin with a factor of one half, rather than requiring identical parent diameters.''',
}

def figure(n):
    return '\n'.join([r'\begin{figure}[tbp]',r'  \centering',
        rf'  \includegraphics[width={"0.98" if n==10 else "1.0"}\linewidth,height=0.78\textheight,keepaspectratio]{{figures/fig{n:02d}.pdf}}',
        r'  \caption{'+CAPTIONS[n]+'}',r'  \label{'+LABELS[n]+'}',r'\end{figure}'])

def section():
    return '\n\n'.join([
r'''\subsection{Demonstration on experimental holograms}

The detector trained on the fixed synthetic ensemble was applied to the reprocessed trajectories from 14 experimental hologram sequences, using three phase-retrieval iterations throughout. No experimental review labels were used to fit or update the detector.
Figure~\ref{fig:experimental_case_study} shows two visually confirmed events with the updated reconstructed images and track hypotheses. The image sequences show two droplets approaching and subsequently persisting as a merged droplet, including transient deformation not represented in the spherical-droplet training simulations.''',figure(10),
r'''Visual review of the top 80 candidates from each sequence yielded 186 confirmed collision--coalescence events, 922 noncollision candidates and 12 ambiguous candidates among 1,120 reviewed candidates. The confirmed-event fraction was 16.6\% using all reviewed candidates and 16.8\% when ambiguous cases were excluded.
Confirmed events were concentrated at high final ranking scores, although noncollision candidates occupied the same score range (Fig.~\ref{fig:experimental_review_score_distribution}). The score is therefore useful for review prioritization but does not by itself establish a definitive collision classification.''',figure(11),
r'''Figure~\ref{fig:experimental_review_budget_operating_points} quantifies the retrospective tradeoff between review effort and recovery of the confirmed-event set.
The top 30 candidates per sequence, or 420 candidates in total, contained 175 of the 186 confirmed events: 94.1\% coverage and 41.7\% precision. This represents a 2.5-fold enrichment relative to the 16.6\% confirmed fraction in the full reviewed cohort, with a loss of 5.9 percentage points in conditional coverage.
Precision crossed 50\% between 24 and 25 candidates per sequence. These budgets required 336 and 350 reviews, respectively, and both contained 173 confirmed events (93.01\% coverage), with precisions of 51.49\% and 49.43\%. Thus 24 was the largest equal integer budget retaining at least 50\% precision. The smallest budget attaining 95\% coverage was 34 per sequence: 177 events among 476 candidates, giving 95.16\% coverage and 37.18\% precision.
These operating points are retrospective results for this reviewed cohort, rather than a prospective guarantee for other sequences. Coverage is conditional on the 186 confirmed events found within the top-80 cohort. Events omitted during candidate generation, unrepresented by the trajectory formulation, or ranked below 80 are not measured by this procedure; absolute experimental recall remains unknown.''',figure(12),
r'''Figure~\ref{fig:experimental_physical_consistency} compares measurements with volume- and momentum-conservation predictions. Of the 186 confirmed events, 184 had both parent diameters and a valid post-event diameter, and 179 also satisfied the three-window velocity availability criterion. One confirmed event had no terminating-track observation at or before raw candidate frame \(f-2\); another had no continuing-track observation after \(f\). Among the five additional exclusions from the velocity diagnostic, four had only one pre-event observation in one parent track, and one had only two post-event observations. These are observed-support limitations under the fixed measurement windows.
For the confirmed events, the median absolute diameter residual was 2.49 \(\mu\mathrm{m}\), and the root-mean-square residual was 19.90 \(\mu\mathrm{m}\). The corresponding velocity values were 0.0139 and 0.0794 \(\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\).
Many confirmed events lay near the one-to-one relationships, but substantial residuals remained. Mean absolute residuals were smaller for confirmed than for noncollision candidates (8.85 versus 28.72 \(\mu\mathrm{m}\) for diameter, and 0.0286 versus 0.0382 \(\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\) for velocity). The velocity root-mean-square residuals, however, were similar between these groups (0.0794 and 0.0799 \(\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\)), because large residuals affected the confirmed set as well. These checks support physical consistency for much of the event set while also revealing limitations in the measurements and track hypotheses; they neither validate every confirmed candidate nor separate the two review classes reliably.
In an additional window-length sensitivity check, requiring at least five observations in every velocity window left 173 confirmed events and gave a velocity root-mean-square residual of 0.0462 \(\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\). This sensitivity check does not replace the original minimum-three-point criterion or remove outliers from Fig.~\ref{fig:experimental_physical_consistency}.''',figure(13),
r'''The confirmed events were also compared with the gravitational collision-kernel predictions in Sec.~\ref{sec:collision_statistics}. Number densities were recomputed from the frame-wise particle measurements, before trajectory filtering. Predictions use all 10,917 frames per sequence, the nominal 10.24-by-10.24-by-102.3 \(\mathrm{mm^3}\) volume, and conditional Poisson counting variability with the measured density and kernel held fixed.
Across all 14 sequences, the volume--time product was \(4.09871\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m^3\,s}\). The 186 confirmed events corresponded to \(4.5380\times10^5\,\mathrm{m^{-3}\,s^{-1}}\), or 83.6\% of the predicted rate of \(5.4264\times10^5\,\mathrm{m^{-3}\,s^{-1}}\). The pooled predicted count was \(222.41\pm14.91\), where the uncertainty is one conditional Poisson standard deviation (Fig.~\ref{fig:experimental_scene_collision_counts}).
Observed counts lay more than one such standard deviation below the predicted mean in five sequences and more than one above in one sequence. These error bars exclude uncertainties in particle measurements, measured density, the collision kernel and missed events; they should not be interpreted as the full uncertainty in agreement between experiment and prediction.''',figure(14),
r'''Figure~\ref{fig:experimental_collision_count_map} assigns 185 confirmed events to parent-diameter pairs. One additional confirmed event lacked one parent diameter, so it contributes to the total count in Fig.~\ref{fig:experimental_scene_collision_counts} but not to the pair map. Events occupied 31 unordered pairs of 25 \(\mu\mathrm{m}\) diameter bins over \(25\leq D<300\,\mu\mathrm{m}\).
The largest cell contained 29 events with smaller-parent diameters of 100--125 \(\mu\mathrm{m}\) and larger-parent diameters of 125--150 \(\mu\mathrm{m}\), compared with 30.27 predicted events. Pairs with the smaller parent between 100 and 150 \(\mu\mathrm{m}\) contained 144 events: 77.4\% of all 186 confirmed events, or 77.8\% of the 185 events assignable to the map. The corresponding prediction was 174.28 events, or 78.4\% of the predicted total.
The broad concentration range was therefore similar, while individual cells differed. The 125--150 \(\mu\mathrm{m}\) same-bin pair contained 7 events versus 9.60 predicted, and the 100--125/150--175 \(\mu\mathrm{m}\) pair contained 20 versus 30.09 predicted. Diameter measurement errors can redistribute observed events among adjacent bins and also affect predicted counts through the estimated number-density products. Counting variability, trajectory errors and missed events provide further possible contributions. Their separate effects are not quantified by the present comparison.
Together, the individual-event diagnostics and the diameter-pair counts demonstrate the acquisition of reviewed collision statistics, but do not establish completeness or provide an efficiency-corrected measurement of the collision kernel.''',figure(15)])+'\n'

ABSTRACT=r'''In 14 experimental hologram sequences recorded in a vertical wind tunnel, visual review of 1,120 candidates yielded 186 confirmed collision--coalescence events, 922 noncollision candidates and 12 ambiguous cases. Reviewing the top 24 candidates per sequence recovered 93.01\% of this confirmed-event set with 51.49\% precision; the top 34 recovered 95.16\% with 37.18\% precision. These coverage values are conditional on the reviewed cohort. Physical-consistency diagnostics showed many events near volume- and momentum-conservation predictions, together with substantial residuals in some events. The measured collision rate was 83.6\% of the gravitational-kernel reference prediction.'''
CONCLUSION=r'''Application to 14 experimental hologram sequences yielded 186 confirmed collision--coalescence events from 1,120 reviewed candidates. The top 24 candidates per sequence contained 173 confirmed events (93.01\% conditional coverage, 51.49\% precision). Increasing the budget to 25 lowered precision to 49.43\% without adding a confirmed event. The minimum budget attaining 95\% coverage was 34 per sequence, containing 177 confirmed events (95.16\% coverage, 37.18\% precision). Valid diameter and velocity diagnostics were available for 184 and 179 confirmed events, respectively. The pooled observed rate was 83.6\% of the gravitational-kernel prediction, and 185 events could be assigned to parent-diameter pairs. These results support reviewed collision statistics and expose measurement limitations; they do not establish absolute experimental recall or completeness.'''
POPULATION=r'''The measured number-density distributions and particle counts are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:exp_data_nd}, and the velocity distributions are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:exp_data_velocity}. These figures summarize the reprocessed experimental measurements used for the present experimental analysis. The synthetic training and evaluation ensemble retains its previously fixed empirical population and velocity settings; it was not regenerated from these updated experimental measurements or review results.'''
